# Real-Time DevOps Project – Jenkins + Nexus Integration

## 1. Project Architecture

GitHub → Jenkins → Maven Build → SonarQube Scan → Quality Gate → Trivy File Scan → Nexus Artifact Upload → Docker Build → Trivy Image Scan → AWS ECR → AWS EKS

## 2. Prerequisites

- Jenkins server installed.
- Maven installed and configured in Jenkins.
- Java installed.
- Nexus Repository Manager installed.
- SonarQube configured.
- Trivy installed on Jenkins build agent.
- Docker installed.
- AWS CLI configured.
- ECR repository created.
- EKS cluster created.
- GitHub repository containing Java application and pom.xml.

## 3. Configure Nexus Repository

1. Open Nexus: http://NEXUS-IP:8081
2. Login as administrator.
3. Navigate to Settings → Repositories.
4. Click Create Repository.
5. Select maven2 (hosted).
6. Configure:

Repository name: maven-releases
Version policy: Release
Layout policy: Strict
Deployment policy: Disable redeploy (production)

7. Create another repository:

Repository name: maven-snapshots
Version policy: Snapshot
Layout policy: Strict
Deployment policy: Allow redeploy

8. Create a dedicated Jenkins service account.
9. Give it the required repository read and upload permissions.

## 4. Configure Jenkins Credentials

Navigate to:

Manage Jenkins → Credentials → System → Global Credentials → Add Credentials.

Create:

Kind: Username with password
Username: jenkins-user
Password: Nexus password
ID: nexus-creds

Also configure the credentials for:
- SonarQube token.
- AWS credentials or EC2 IAM role.
- GitHub credentials if the repository is private.

## 5. Configure pom.xml

Add the following inside the pom.xml:

<distributionManagement>
    <repository>
        <id>nexus-releases</id>
        <url>http://NEXUS-PRIVATE-IP:8081/repository/maven-releases/</url>
    </repository>

    <snapshotRepository>
        <id>nexus-snapshots</id>
        <url>http://NEXUS-PRIVATE-IP:8081/repository/maven-snapshots/</url>
    </snapshotRepository>
</distributionManagement>

Use a Maven settings.xml on the Jenkins build agent:

<settings>
    <servers>
        <server>
            <id>nexus-releases</id>
            <username>${env.NEXUS_USERNAME}</username>
            <password>${env.NEXUS_PASSWORD}</password>
        </server>
        <server>
            <id>nexus-snapshots</id>
            <username>${env.NEXUS_USERNAME}</username>
            <password>${env.NEXUS_PASSWORD}</password>
        </server>
    </servers>
</settings>

The IDs in settings.xml must match the IDs in pom.xml.

## 6. Configure Jenkins Pipeline

Create a Jenkinsfile in your GitHub project.

Configure the following stages:

1. Git Checkout
   - Download source code from GitHub.

2. Maven Build
   - Execute: mvn clean package
   - Generate the JAR artifact.

3. SonarQube Scan
   - Scan source code.
   - Generate code quality analysis.

4. Quality Gate
   - Check whether the project meets the configured quality requirements.

5. Trivy File Scan
   - Scan the filesystem and dependencies for vulnerabilities.

6. Nexus Artifact Upload
   - Use Jenkins Nexus credentials.
   - Execute: mvn deploy
   - Upload the JAR artifact to Nexus.

7. Docker Build
   - Create a Docker image using the generated JAR file.

8. Trivy Image Scan
   - Scan the Docker image for vulnerabilities.

9. Push Docker Image to AWS ECR
   - Authenticate Docker with ECR.
   - Tag the image.
   - Push the image to ECR.

10. Deploy to AWS EKS
    - Update the Kubernetes deployment with the new ECR image.
    - Verify the deployment and pods.

## 7. Important Maven Commands

Build the application:

mvn clean package

Upload artifact to Nexus:

mvn deploy

Download dependencies:

mvn dependency:resolve

## 8. Verify Nexus Artifact

Open Nexus Dashboard.

Navigate to:

Browse → maven-releases

Verify that your JAR file is available.

Example:

com/example/banking-app/1.0/banking-app-1.0.jar

## 9. Understand Artifact Storage

GitHub:
Stores source code.

Maven:
Builds and packages Java application.

Nexus:
Stores JAR, WAR and other application packages.

SonarQube:
Stores code quality analysis results.

Trivy:
Scans files and container images for vulnerabilities.

Docker:
Builds container images.

AWS ECR:
Stores Docker images.

AWS EKS:
Runs the application containers.

## 10. Real-Time Pipeline Flow

Developer pushes code to GitHub.

Jenkins detects the change and starts the pipeline.

Jenkins downloads source code.

Maven builds the application and generates a JAR.

SonarQube scans the source code.

Quality Gate validates the code quality.

Trivy scans the project files and dependencies.

Maven deploy uploads the JAR to Nexus.

Docker builds an image using the JAR.

Trivy scans the Docker image.

Jenkins pushes the Docker image to AWS ECR.

Jenkins deploys the image to AWS EKS.

Kubernetes starts the application pods.

## 11. Production Best Practices

- Use private IP communication between Jenkins and Nexus.
- Never expose Nexus deployment credentials in Jenkinsfile.
- Use Jenkins Credentials.
- Use HTTPS for production Nexus access.
- Use unique Maven release versions.
- Do not overwrite released artifacts.
- Use IAM roles instead of hardcoded AWS access keys.
- Configure Trivy to fail the pipeline based on vulnerability severity.
- Store scan reports as Jenkins build artifacts or in a dedicated reporting system.
- Maintain separate repositories for releases and snapshots.
- Use immutable Docker image tags instead of latest.
- Configure backups for Nexus storage.
